    {
      "question": "e-12539",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "1c4faf7cef34d6a18b75aa1c9d625093e7862b50534c100eddcd8c960523dfac",
      "new_sha256": "dbfb6217b1201b46dc5a059e096bd8e4ee4a331b29d313ae53c1440494d72803",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-12539",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation and let $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretch. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Q & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $QP\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QP & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062905/example-12539.json",
          "file_sha256": "32911049ad04b2b127d07f87fe42dcda68912ef25320441b5c4a10fd50eb8605"
        }
